window.DOCS_INDEX=[{"p":"Quick start","h":"Quick start","u":"getting-started.html#quick-start","x":"Hit feedback on your own enemy in about five minutes."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"1. Add Hit Feedback","u":"getting-started.html#1-add-hit-feedback","x":"Select your enemy (in the Hierarchy, or a prefab in the Project window) and open Tools > All In One Hit Feedback Kit > Setup Wizard . Pick a Preset ( Existing Game (safe) if your game already has combat) and press Add Hit Feedback . Prefabs get a new variant in Assets/HitFeedbackKitData/Prefabs ; your original prefab is never changed. You can also just add the component: Add Component > All In One Hit Feedback Kit > Hit Feedback . It works at once with the Action RPG preset."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"2. Hit it","u":"getting-started.html#2-hit-it","x":"Pick whichever fits your game: One line of code , from your weapon, projectile or combat script: using RomanoAssets.HitFeedbackKit; HitFeedback.Hit(enemy, 25); // a normal hit of 25 HitFeedback.Hit(enemy, new HitInfo(60, HitKind.Critical).At(point)); // a critical hit where it landed Your own health script , no code: the Setup Wizard finds it and adds a Hit Feedback Bridge that watches its health value. See Hook it into your game . The kit's Health component (Setup Wizard: Add the kit's basic Health). Call health.Damage(25) and the feedback plays by itself, including the kill moment when healt"},{"p":"Quick start","h":"3. Press Play","u":"getting-started.html#3-press-play","x":"Hit it. You see the number, the flash, the freeze, the shake and the push. Use the buttons in the Hit Feedback inspector ( Hit , Critical , Heal , Kill ) to try it without any code."},{"p":"Quick start","h":"4. Make it feel like your game","u":"getting-started.html#4-make-it-feel-like-your-game","x":"Change the Preset for a different feel, or press Save As Profile to change every value. Drag the Juice slider for more or less of everything. Add Player Hit Feedback to your player for a damage vignette, direction arrows and rumble. Nothing happens? Open Tools > All In One Hit Feedback Kit > Why No Feedback? . It lists every part of each hit, what played and why anything was skipped, with a fix."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"Setup Wizard","u":"setup-wizard.html#setup-wizard","x":"Tools > All In One Hit Feedback Kit > Setup Wizard adds Hit Feedback to your own enemies, players and props, one or a whole folder at once. It also opens from the right-click menu in the Hierarchy and the Project window ( All In One Hit Feedback Kit > Add Hit Feedback (Setup Wizard) )."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"1. What to set up","u":"setup-wizard.html#1-what-to-set-up","x":"Select objects and press Use Selection . You can pick: prefabs in the Project window, a folder : every prefab in it, objects in the scene. For each one the wizard shows what it found: the renderers it will flash (meshes, skinned meshes, sprites), what will be pushed (Rigidbody, Rigidbody 2D, Character Controller, NavMesh Agent or the transform), whether an Animator will pause in hitstop, and whether it already has health. Problems are listed under it, for example \"Marked Static\" (static batched objects can't flash) or \"Has missing scripts\" (reimport it first)."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"2. The feel","u":"setup-wizard.html#2-the-feel","x":"Pick a Preset , or drag in a profile you already made. You can change everything later. Adding to a game that already has combat? Pick Existing Game (safe) : numbers, flash, bar, sound and sparks, and nothing that moves, freezes or scales your objects or touches your camera."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"3. Who controls health?","u":"setup-wizard.html#3-who-controls-health","x":"Find it for me (recommended): looks for your own health script by name (Health, HP, Damageable, Stats...). When it finds one it adds a Hit Feedback Bridge that watches its health value, so your game stays in charge. When there is none it adds the kit's Health. It never adds a second health system next to yours. My game already has health : always adds a Bridge (watching your health script when one is found; otherwise connect your events to it, see Hook it into your game ). Add the kit's basic Health with a Max health : the kit's Health component, which plays feedback by itself on every change."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"Extras","u":"setup-wizard.html#extras","x":"This is the player : adds Player Hit Feedback (vignette, direction arrows, low-health pulse, rumble)."},{"p":"Setup Wizard","h":"4. Add it","u":"setup-wizard.html#4-add-it","x":"Add Hit Feedback : Prefabs get a new Prefab Variant called \"Name (Hit Feedback)\" in Assets/HitFeedbackKitData/Prefabs . Your original prefab is not changed (a test checks its file stays byte-identical) and still receives your edits and pack updates. Use the variant in your scenes. Scene objects get the component directly. Undo works. Prefabs with missing scripts are skipped; reimport them and try again."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Hook it into your game","u":"integration.html#hook-it-into-your-game","x":"Your game already decides who gets hurt, how much and when they die. The kit only shows it. This page is about connecting the two without changing how your game works."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Pick one route","u":"integration.html#pick-one-route","x":"Your game has... Use Code? A health script with a health number (a field or property) Hit Feedback Bridge, Watch None A health script with UnityEvents (OnDamaged, OnHealthChanged, OnDied) Hit Feedback Bridge, events None A place in code where damage is dealt HitFeedback.Hit(target, amount) One line No health at all yet The kit's Health component None Projectiles, hitboxes or traps but no damage code The Hit On Contact recipe None Use one route per enemy. Two routes (for example a Bridge watching your health and a call to HitFeedback.Hit in your damage code) show every hit twice. The Setup Wiza"},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"The Existing Game preset","u":"integration.html#the-existing-game-preset","x":"Start with Existing Game (safe) when your game already has combat. It shows numbers, the flash, the bar, sound and sparks, and nothing else: no knockback, no squash, no hitstop, no camera shake, no slow motion, and the kill moment never hides or destroys your object. Your movement, physics, camera and time are never touched. Once that works, turn on the extras you want one at a time in the profile."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Hit Feedback Bridge","u":"integration.html#hit-feedback-bridge","x":"Add Hit Feedback Bridge next to Hit Feedback (Add Component > All In One Hit Feedback Kit). Watch a value (no code). Press Find my health script , or drag your health script into Health script and pick its health value from the list (any float, int or double field or property). Optionally pick its max health value for the bar. Then every drop plays a hit, reaching Dead at or below plays the kill, a rise shows healing, and the bar follows the value. Coming back from zero (a respawn) shows nothing and tidies the object up. The Bridge only reads: it never changes your health. Reading it every fra"},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Recipes","u":"integration.html#recipes","x":""},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Your own health script, in code","u":"integration.html#your-own-health-script-in-code","x":"public void TakeDamage(float amount, Vector3 point, Transform attacker) { hp -= amount; HitFeedback.Hit(gameObject, new HitInfo(amount).At(point).By(attacker).Killing(hp <= 0)); } Add IHealthSource to the same script (four properties, see Code, events and your own UI ) and the bars follow your health too."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Projectiles, 3D and 2D","u":"integration.html#projectiles-3d-and-2d","x":"Put Hit On Contact (Recipes) on the bullet. It works with collisions and triggers in 3D and 2D, uses the exact contact point and the bullet's direction, and can destroy the bullet on its first hit. Turn on Feedback only when your own script deals the damage. From your own projectile code: void OnCollisionEnter(Collision c) { HitFeedback.Hit(c.collider, new HitInfo(damage).At(c.GetContact(0).point).From(-c.relativeVelocity)); } HitFeedback.Hit takes the collider you touched: it finds the Hit Feedback on it or on a parent, so hitting an arm collider on a character works."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Melee hitboxes and trigger zones","u":"integration.html#melee-hitboxes-and-trigger-zones","x":"Hit On Contact on the hitbox. Each target is hit once per Once every seconds, so a hitbox that stays inside an enemy for several frames, or touches three of its colliders, gives one hit."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Hitscan weapons","u":"integration.html#hitscan-weapons","x":"Call Hitscan Hit 's Fire() (or FireShot from a UnityEvent). It casts a ray forward and plays the hit at the point struck. Or in your own code: HitFeedback.Hit(rayHit.collider, new HitInfo(damage).At(rayHit.point).From(ray.direction)) ."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Visual Scripting","u":"integration.html#visual-scripting","x":"Every Bridge method and HitFeedback.HitAmount , CriticalHit , HealAmount , KillingHit take one number, so they show up as plain nodes. In a Script Graph, get the Hit Feedback (or Bridge) component and call the method with your damage."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Pooled enemies","u":"integration.html#pooled-enemies","x":"Nothing to do: Hit Feedback puts the object back (colour, scale, frozen state) when it is disabled and enabled again. If you reuse an object without disabling it, call hitFeedback.ResetVisual() when you respawn it."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Ragdolls","u":"integration.html#ragdolls","x":"The Setup Wizard spots ragdolls (three or more jointed bones) and turns off knockback and squash for them, because physics owns the body. The flash, numbers, bar, sound and sparks still play. Put Hit Feedback on the root; hits on any bone find it."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Many renderers, LODs and custom shaders","u":"integration.html#many-renderers-lods-and-custom-shaders","x":"The flash draws a copy of each renderer with the kit's own shader, so it shows whatever your material is, custom shaders included, and never changes your materials. The copy is solid: it doesn't follow alpha cutouts, dissolves or vertex animation done in a shader. Only the first LOD flashes. To choose exactly what flashes (skip a weapon, a shadow blob or a VFX mesh), fill in Renderers on Hit Feedback."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Your own camera, Cinemachine and camera rigs","u":"integration.html#your-own-camera-cinemachine-and-camera-rigs","x":"Shake moves the camera a little after everything else and puts it back before your scripts run, so camera follow scripts keep working. With Cinemachine 3, tick Use Cinemachine in the profile's shake to send impulses instead (your Cinemachine camera needs an Impulse Listener). When Camera.main is not the camera players see (a rig, several cameras), add Hit Feedback Camera to the right one. Split screen with one shake per player is not supported in 1.0."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Multiplayer","u":"integration.html#multiplayer","x":"Play feedback where it is seen: on every client, from the health value your network code already syncs. Watch that value with the Bridge, or call bridge.HealthChanged(newValue) from your sync callback. Don't send feedback over the network yourself, and don't play it on the server only."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"ScriptableObject event channels","u":"integration.html#scriptableobject-event-channels","x":"Add a listener for your channel on the enemy and point its response at the Bridge's PlayDamage or HealthChanged ."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Your TextMesh Pro font","u":"integration.html#your-textmesh-pro-font","x":"Add TextMesh Pro Numbers to any object in the scene and drag in your TMP font asset: numbers then use your font and material. See Damage numbers ."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"Big frameworks and character controllers","u":"integration.html#big-frameworks-and-character-controllers","x":"Most combat frameworks have either a health value (use Watch ) or damage and death events (use events ). If their health lives in a system rather than a field, add a small script that listens to their event and calls the Bridge. Choose the Existing Game preset so knockback and hitstop don't fight their movement. We test against plain Unity components, not against each framework."},{"p":"Hook it into your game","h":"When nothing shows","u":"integration.html#when-nothing-shows","x":"Open Tools > All In One Hit Feedback Kit > Why No Feedback? . It lists every hit, says which effects played and why the others didn't, and names objects you hit that have no Hit Feedback. The Hit Feedback inspector also says whether your game has sent it any hits yet (Preview Hit doesn't count)."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Demo scenes","u":"samples.html#demo-scenes","x":"Open them from Tools > All In One Hit Feedback Kit > Open Demo . They play with keyboard and mouse or a gamepad, with either input system. The Showcase and 2D demos have a Make it yours panel (M or Tab shows and hides it). Every change applies to every enemy at once, including the next wave: a banner names the new look and the enemies on screen take a test hit so you see it straight away. Hit all (or H ) plays a test hit whenever you like. You can change the preset (keys 1 to 7), how numbers show, move and are written, their colour and size, the flash mode and colour, hitstop and its length, s"},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Showcase: the arena","u":"samples.html#showcase-the-arena","x":"Three waves of slimes, then the King Slime with a boss bar. Move with WASD or the left stick, swing your sword with the left mouse button, J or the gamepad's west button. It shows every part of a hit together: numbers that combine, the flash on a skinned slime, hitstop, camera shake and punch, knockback, squash, floating bars, sounds, sparks and the kill moment. When a slime hits you, you see the player side: the red vignette, an arrow pointing at it, your HUD bar draining, and rumble on a gamepad. Keys 1 to 7 switch every enemy to another preset while you play."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"Feature Gallery","u":"samples.html#feature-gallery","x":"Seven training dummies, one per preset, hit automatically so you can compare them side by side. Click a dummy to hit it yourself; the panel picks what a click plays (hit, critical, heal, shield, block, miss, poison, a status word, or a killing blow). The accessibility settings (Reduce Motion, flash safety, colour-blind safe colours, text size) and the Juice slider are on the panel too."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"2D Side View","u":"samples.html#2d-side-view","x":"A side-view level with sprites and Rigidbody 2D. A/D or the arrows to move, Space to jump, click, J or the west button to swing. Sprite flash, 2D knockback, squash and stretch on sprites, and the player HUD bar."},{"p":"Demo scenes","h":"What the demos use","u":"samples.html#what-the-demos-use","x":"Everything in the demos is made by Romano Assets except the hit sounds, which come from Kenney's Impact Sounds and RPG Audio (CC0; see Third-Party Notices.txt). The demo scripts ( Samples/Scripts ) are examples only: your game uses its own movement, combat and input."},{"p":"Hit Feedback and hitting things","h":"Hit Feedback and hitting things","u":"hit-feedback.html#hit-feedback-and-hitting-things","x":"Hit Feedback is the one component the kit needs. Put it on anything that can be hit: an enemy, the player, a crate. Every hit then plays the whole reaction from its profile."},{"p":"Hit Feedback and hitting things","h":"Hitting it","u":"hit-feedback.html#hitting-it","x":"Way How One line of code HitFeedback.Hit(target, 25); finds the Hit Feedback on the target or its nearest parent, so passing the collider you hit works. Returns false when there is none (the debugger remembers it). A full hit HitFeedback.Hit(target, new HitInfo(25, HitKind.Critical).At(point).By(attacker)); The kit's Health health.Damage(25) , health.Damage(hitInfo) , health.Heal(10) , health.Kill() . Feedback plays by itself. UnityEvents and animation events Methods with one float: HitAmount , CriticalHit , HealAmount , ShieldHit , DamageOverTime , KillingHit . And Miss , Block , ShowStatus ("},{"p":"Hit Feedback and hitting things","h":"HitInfo: one hit","u":"hit-feedback.html#hitinfo-one-hit","x":"Field What it does Amount The number shown. Kind Normal, Critical, Heal, Shield, Miss, Block, DamageOverTime or Status. Each kind has its own look and strength. IsKill ( .Killing() ) The killing blow: plays the kill moment. Point ( .At(point) ) Where it landed: numbers (Hit Point anchor) and particles start there. Direction ( .From(dir) ) or Source ( .By(attacker) ) Which way it travelled: knockback, camera punch and the player's damage arrow use it. Without either, the kit pushes away from the camera. Text A word instead of the number (\"Stunned\"). Icon An icon beside the number. Strength Scal"},{"p":"Hit Feedback and hitting things","h":"What each kind plays","u":"hit-feedback.html#what-each-kind-plays","x":"Kind Plays Normal, Critical, Shield, Block Everything (criticals stronger, blocks softer). Heal Number (green, with a +), flash, squash, bar, sound. No push, shake or freeze. Damage over time Small combining numbers, a light flash, bar, sound. No freeze, shake or push. Miss, Status The word and its sound only."},{"p":"Hit Feedback and hitting things","h":"The component","u":"hit-feedback.html#the-component","x":"Setting What it does Profile / Preset The feel. Without a profile asset the preset's shared profile is used; Save As Profile makes an asset you can change. Juice (with a profile asset) More or less of everything. Renderers to flash Empty: every mesh, skinned mesh and sprite under the object (lower LOD levels and children with their own Hit Feedback are skipped). Visual (squash) What squashes and pops. Use a child when the root also moves or scales. Number and bar anchor Where numbers and the bar appear. Empty: above the renderers. Health source Your own health script (anything with IHealthSour"},{"p":"Hit Feedback and hitting things","h":"Preview in the inspector","u":"hit-feedback.html#preview-in-the-inspector","x":"In Play Mode the Hit , Critical , Heal and Kill buttons play on the selected objects, and the inspector lists what the last hit played and why anything was skipped."},{"p":"Profiles, presets and Juice","h":"Profiles, presets and Juice","u":"profiles.html#profiles-presets-and-juice","x":"A Hit Feedback Profile holds everything a hit plays. Share one between all your goblins, another for bosses."},{"p":"Profiles, presets and Juice","h":"Make one","u":"profiles.html#make-one","x":"Assets > Create > All In One Hit Feedback Kit > Profile from Preset > (preset) : a new profile already set to that feel. Or press Save As Profile on a Hit Feedback that uses a preset (saved in Assets/HitFeedbackKitData/Profiles )."},{"p":"Profiles, presets and Juice","h":"The presets","u":"profiles.html#the-presets","x":"Preset Feel Arcade Big, bright and bouncy, with strong shake and hitstop. Kill pop and a screen flash. Action RPG Clear numbers that add up, crits that pop, a bar on hit. Souls-like Heavy and grounded: a global hitstop, rim flash, slow drain, quiet numbers at the hit point. Cartoon Squash and stretch, bouncy numbers ending in \"!\", bright colours, sparks and puffs. Shooter Small fast numbers at the hit point, a rim flash, light shake, no freeze. Idle and Mobile Many numbers that add up, short forms (1.2K), bigger numbers for bigger amounts, no shake. Minimal A clean flash and a small number, no"},{"p":"Profiles, presets and Juice","h":"Juice","u":"profiles.html#juice","x":"One slider for everything. 0 keeps only numbers, bars and sounds; 1 is the preset; 2 doubles flash, shake, hitstop, knockback, squash and particles. There is also a master Juice for the whole game in HitFeedbackSettings.Juice , for an options menu."},{"p":"Profiles, presets and Juice","h":"Scale with amount","u":"profiles.html#scale-with-amount","x":"Turn on Scale with amount and bigger hits feel bigger: shake, hitstop, knockback and squash scale with the amount compared with Full strength amount . (Number size by amount is a separate setting under Numbers.)"},{"p":"Profiles, presets and Juice","h":"Kinds","u":"profiles.html#kinds","x":"Each kind (Normal, Critical, Heal, Shield, Miss, Block, Damage Over Time, Status) has a colour, a colour-blind safe colour, a word, a suffix, a size, a strength, an icon and its sounds."},{"p":"Profiles, presets and Juice","h":"The sections","u":"profiles.html#the-sections","x":"Numbers, Flash, Hitstop, Camera shake and punch, Knockback, Squash, Floating health bar, Sound, Particles and Kill moment. Each is on its own page here. The inspector shows a live preview of the numbers as you change them."},{"p":"Damage numbers","h":"Damage numbers","u":"damage-numbers.html#damage-numbers","x":"Numbers and words drawn on top of the finished frame (after post effects, so bloom never smears them), the same in every render pipeline. They are pooled and steady play makes no garbage."},{"p":"Damage numbers","h":"Settings (Profile > Numbers)","u":"damage-numbers.html#settings-profile--numbers","x":"Setting What it does Display as Number, Number And Icon, Icon, Word (the kind's word), None. Also per object under Overrides. Motion Rise, Arc (pops up and falls to a side), Punch (pops big and settles), Slide, Stay. Space Screen: always on top, the same size at any distance. World: sized by distance, and can hide behind walls. Anchor Above the target (above its bar), at the hit point, or at its middle. Plus an offset. Size Text height in pixels at 1080p; scales with the screen, each kind's size and the player's text size. Font, Bold Any font asset in your project; empty uses Unity's built-in "},{"p":"Damage numbers","h":"Per kind (Profile > Kinds)","u":"damage-numbers.html#per-kind-profile--kinds","x":"Colour, colour-blind safe colour, word (\"Miss\", \"Blocked\"), suffix (\"!\" makes 123!), size, icon."},{"p":"Damage numbers","h":"Numbers anywhere","u":"damage-numbers.html#numbers-anywhere","x":"DamageNumbers.Show(position, 25, HitKind.Heal); // a number, Action RPG style or a profile you pass DamageNumbers.ShowText(position, \"Level up!\", HitKind.Status, myProfile); DamageNumbers.Hidden = true; // hide them (for a cut scene)"},{"p":"Damage numbers","h":"Your TextMesh Pro font","u":"damage-numbers.html#your-textmesh-pro-font","x":"With TextMesh Pro in your project (it comes with Unity 6's UI package), add TextMesh Pro Numbers (Add Component > All In One Hit Feedback Kit) to any object in the scene and drag in your TMP font asset and, if you like, a material preset (outline, glow, gradient). While it is enabled every number is shown with TextMesh Pro in your font, in world space, facing the camera; kinds, colours, words, formatting and sizes still come from your profiles. Turn it off and the kit draws its own numbers again. In 1.0 it shows one number per hit (it doesn't add hits into one growing number)."},{"p":"Damage numbers","h":"Your own text (UI Toolkit, your prefab)","u":"damage-numbers.html#your-own-text-ui-toolkit-your-prefab","x":"Set DamageNumbers.CustomDisplay to your own object implementing IDamageNumberDisplay . The kit hands it every number with its text already worked out (\"1.2K\", \"+25\", \"Miss\"), its colour, size and icon. See Code ."},{"p":"Damage numbers","h":"When numbers are hidden","u":"damage-numbers.html#when-numbers-are-hidden","x":"The screen layer hides while the game is paused (Time.timeScale at 0 and not set by the kit's own hitstop) and when HitFeedbackOverlay.Visible is false, for example while your own menu is open. Numbers from a scene are cleared when a new scene loads on its own."},{"p":"Hit flash","h":"Hit flash","u":"hit-flash.html#hit-flash","x":"The target flashes a colour for a moment. It works on meshes, skinned meshes and sprites, with any material and any shader, in Built-in, URP and HDRP."},{"p":"Hit flash","h":"How it works (and why your materials are safe)","u":"hit-flash.html#how-it-works-and-why-your-materials-are-safe","x":"On the first hit the kit makes a copy of each renderer (sharing its mesh, bones or sprite) drawn with the kit's own flash shader just in front of the original. The copy shows only while flashing. Your materials, shaders and material slots are never changed, and a mesh with several materials flashes all of them. Skipped, with the reason shown in Why No Feedback?: static batched objects (their mesh is merged into one), particle, trail and line renderers, and lower LOD levels (the first LOD is flashed). If you add renderers at runtime, call RebuildFlash() ."},{"p":"Hit flash","h":"Settings (Profile > Flash)","u":"hit-flash.html#settings-profile--flash","x":"Setting What it does Mode Fill (the whole object), Rim (only the edges glow; good on large or detailed models), Fill And Rim. Color The flash colour for normal hits. Use kind colors Gold criticals, blue shield hits and so on. Heals always flash their own green. Duration, Strength How long (real time, so it shows during hitstop) and how strong. Blinks Blink this many times instead of one flash: the classic invulnerability blink. Rim width Higher is a thinner edge. Per object: Overrides > Flash Color."},{"p":"Hit flash","h":"Flash safety","u":"hit-flash.html#flash-safety","x":"With flash safety on (the default), an object flashes at full strength at most three times a second; extra flashes in that second are dimmed. See Accessibility ."},{"p":"Hit flash","h":"HDRP","u":"hit-flash.html#hdrp","x":"The flash is drawn in HDRP's unlit pass, before tone mapping, so it follows your exposure like everything else in the frame."},{"p":"Hitstop, shake, knockback and squash","h":"Hitstop, shake, knockback and squash","u":"hitstop-shake-knockback.html#hitstop-shake-knockback-and-squash","x":"Every one of these puts things back exactly afterwards: the object's velocity, the game's time scale, your camera, the object's scale."},{"p":"Hitstop, shake, knockback and squash","h":"Hitstop (Profile > Hitstop)","u":"hitstop-shake-knockback.html#hitstop-profile--hitstop","x":"Setting What it does Mode Target Only: just the hit object pauses (its Animators, Rigidbodies, Rigidbody 2Ds, particle systems, NavMesh Agent and your own scripts with IHitstopReceiver). Global: the whole game freezes for a moment. Off. Duration Real seconds for a normal hit (0.05 is about three frames). Each kind's strength scales it. Max duration The longest a single stop may last. A second hit while frozen extends the freeze up to this, never stacks past it. Freeze attacker Target Only: the attacker (the hit's Source, when it has Hit Feedback) freezes for the same moment. Global time scale "},{"p":"Hitstop, shake, knockback and squash","h":"Camera shake and punch (Profile > Camera shake and punch)","u":"hitstop-shake-knockback.html#camera-shake-and-punch-profile--camera-shake-and-punch","x":"Trauma based: each hit adds trauma, the shake is trauma squared (small hits stay subtle, big ones build up) and it fades smoothly. Setting What it does Trauma How much a normal hit adds (0 to 1). Max offset, Max angle, Frequency, Recovery The feel of the shake. Punch A quick nudge in the hit's direction. FOV kick A quick zoom (on 2D orthographic cameras, the same as a size change). Max distance Hits farther from the camera shake less, down to nothing. Use Cinemachine Cinemachine 3 impulses instead (your camera needs a Cinemachine Impulse Listener). It never fights your camera controller. The s"},{"p":"Hitstop, shake, knockback and squash","h":"Knockback (Profile > Knockback)","u":"hitstop-shake-knockback.html#knockback-profile--knockback","x":"Mover What happens Rigidbody A push (velocity change, or an impulse when Ignore Mass is off). Rigidbody 2D A push along the hit, with an upward part. Character Controller A short glide using Move, so it respects collisions. NavMesh Agent A short glide using the agent's Move. Nothing (transform) A short glide of the transform, when Move Transform is on. Force, Upward, Duration (for glides), Ignore Mass, Move Transform. The direction comes from the hit's Direction, its Source, its Point, or else away from the camera."},{"p":"Hitstop, shake, knockback and squash","h":"Squash (Profile > Squash)","u":"hitstop-shake-knockback.html#squash-profile--squash","x":"Scale Punch, Squash And Stretch, or Wobble, by an amount and for a duration. It works on the Visual you set (a child, so the root's own scale and collider stay as they are) and always ends at the exact starting scale. If your animation also scales the object, the kit squashes on top of it."},{"p":"Health and health bars","h":"Health and health bars","u":"health-bars.html#health-and-health-bars","x":""},{"p":"Health and health bars","h":"Where health comes from","u":"health-bars.html#where-health-comes-from","x":"Bars show the health of the first of these the object has: Your own health script with IHealthSource (four properties: CurrentHealth, MaxHealth, CurrentShield, MaxShield). Drag it into Hit Feedback > Health source, or just put it on the same object. The kit's Health component (optional, basic). Values you set with hitFeedback.SetHealth(current, max, shield, maxShield) ."},{"p":"Health and health bars","h":"The Health component","u":"health-bars.html#the-health-component","x":"Max health, start health, an optional shield that takes damage first, an invulnerable time after each hit, and events (On Damaged, On Healed, On Died). Every change plays feedback on the object's Hit Feedback: a damage number, a \"Shield\" hit when the shield takes it all, a green heal, and the kill moment when health reaches zero. health.Damage(25); health.Damage(new HitInfo(40, HitKind.Critical).At(point).By(attacker)); health.Heal(10); health.AddShield(20); health.Kill(); health.Revive(); // full health again; a kill pop is undone"},{"p":"Health and health bars","h":"Floating bar (Profile > Floating health bar)","u":"health-bars.html#floating-bar-profile--floating-health-bar","x":"Setting What it does Visibility On Hit (then fades), Always, While Damaged, Never. Show time, Fade time How long it stays after a hit and how it fades. Width, Height, Offset Pixels at 1080p, and metres above the target. Numbers start above the bar, so they never overlap it. Fill, Low, Drain, Back, Shield, Border colours The look. Below Low Health the fill turns the low colour. Drain delay, Drain speed The lost part waits, then drains away. Segment size A tick every this much health. Rounded Round ends. Per object: Overrides > Health Bar (off for a boss that has a boss bar). For one character's"},{"p":"Health and health bars","h":"Boss bar and player HUD bar: Screen Health Bar","u":"health-bars.html#boss-bar-and-player-hud-bar-screen-health-bar","x":"Add Screen Health Bar to the boss or the player: Boss : wide, at the top centre, with a name. Turns on with the boss. Player HUD : compact, bottom left, with \"350 / 500\" inside. Custom : your own anchor (0,0 bottom left to 1,1 top right), offset and size. It has its own bar settings (colours, delayed drain, segments, shield), shows the numbers, can hide while full, and hides when the target is defeated."},{"p":"Player feedback","h":"Player feedback","u":"player-feedback.html#player-feedback","x":"Add Player Hit Feedback to your player (next to its Hit Feedback or Health). It reacts to the player's hits by itself; or call PlayerHit(hitInfo) from your own code. Part What it does Settings Damage vignette The screen edges flash a colour; bigger hits come closer to the full strength. Colour, strength, time Damage direction An arrow around the screen centre points at where the hit came from (its Source, Direction or Point). Colour, time, radius, size Low health The screen edges pulse while health is below a threshold (a steady glow with Reduce Motion). Threshold, colour, rate, strength Rumbl"},{"p":"Sound, particles and the kill moment","h":"Sound, particles and the kill moment","u":"sound-particles-kill.html#sound-particles-and-the-kill-moment","x":""},{"p":"Sound, particles and the kill moment","h":"Sound (Profile > Sound, clips under Kinds)","u":"sound-particles-kill.html#sound-profile--sound-clips-under-kinds","x":"Each kind has its own clips; one is picked at random. Kinds without clips use the Normal kind's (except Miss and Status). Setting What it does Volume, Pitch min and max Random pitch for variety. Combo pitch step Hits that combine into one number raise the pitch a little each time. Cooldown The shortest time between two sounds from the same target, so rapid hits don't stack. Spatial blend 0: the same everywhere. 1: from the target's position. Output An Audio Mixer group. Sounds play from a pool of 16 voices shared by every target; the master volume is HitFeedbackSettings.Volume ."},{"p":"Sound, particles and the kill moment","h":"Particles (Profile > Particles)","u":"sound-particles-kill.html#particles-profile--particles","x":"The kit's own Sparks (bright, stretched, falling), a Puff (soft, rising), both, or none. Count, size, speed, colour (or each kind's colour). They play where the hit landed and fly back along the hit. Add your own prefab (any particle effect) and it is spawned at the hit and returned to a pool after its lifetime."},{"p":"Sound, particles and the kill moment","h":"Kill moment (Profile > Kill moment)","u":"sound-particles-kill.html#kill-moment-profile--kill-moment","x":"On the killing blow ( HitInfo.IsKill , KillingHit , or Health reaching zero): Setting What it does Slow motion, Slow motion time The game slows for a moment (safe: see Hitstop). Number size The final number is bigger. Extra shake, Extra hitstop A harder last hit. Screen flash, colour A quick full-screen flash (dimmed by flash safety when kills come fast). Pop and fade, Pop time The target pops up and shrinks away. After, After delay Nothing (your code takes over), Disable or Destroy the object. Pooling enemies? A popped object comes back at its own size when you enable it again or call health."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Make it yours","u":"make-it-yours.html#make-it-yours","x":"Everything a player sees, hears or feels can be changed or switched off, without code."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Start from a preset","u":"make-it-yours.html#start-from-a-preset","x":"Eight presets change every part at once (see Profiles ). Then the Juice slider for more or less of everything. The demos' Make it yours panel (M) switches presets and the main choices while you play."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Every part can be switched off","u":"make-it-yours.html#every-part-can-be-switched-off","x":"In the profile each part has its own switch (Numbers, Flash, Hitstop mode, Shake, Knockback, Squash, Bar visibility, Sound, Particles, Kill moment). Per object, Overrides turns any part on or off for that object only, and gives it its own flash colour, number colour and size, and floating bar style (a boss with a wide gold bar and big gold numbers, minions with the default)."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"The look","u":"make-it-yours.html#the-look","x":"What Where Number style: Display as, motion, size, font, bold, outline, shadow, shine, format Profile > Numbers Each kind's colour, colour-blind colour, word, suffix, size, icon Profile > Kinds Where numbers appear: above the target, at the hit point, the middle, an offset, your own anchor Profile > Numbers, Hit Feedback > Number and bar anchor Flash mode, colour, blinks Profile > Flash, Overrides > Flash Color One character's numbers and bar Overrides > Number Color, Number Scale, Override Health Bar + Health Bar Style Bar colours, size, rounded ends, segments, drain Profile > Floating health"},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Your own text and UI","u":"make-it-yours.html#your-own-text-and-ui","x":"Your own font : drag any font asset into Profile > Numbers > Font. Your TextMesh Pro font : add TextMesh Pro Numbers and drag in your TMP font asset. Your own number display (UI Toolkit, your prefab): DamageNumbers.CustomDisplay . Your own bars and HUD : read health from IHealthSource , react to HitFeedback.AnyHit , OnHit , OnKilled , and set the kit's bars to Never. Your own words in any language: HitFeedbackSettings.Translate ."},{"p":"Make it yours","h":"Left as they are on purpose","u":"make-it-yours.html#left-as-they-are-on-purpose","x":"The Why No Feedback? window's look (an editor tool, not seen by players) and the demo panels (examples only)."},{"p":"Accessibility","h":"Accessibility","u":"accessibility.html#accessibility","x":"Built in, and meant for your game's options menu. Set the starting values in Project Settings > All In One Hit Feedback Kit (saved in Assets/HitFeedbackKitData/Resources ), and change them while playing through HitFeedbackSettings : Setting What it does ReduceMotion No camera shake, punch or zoom kick, no global hitstop or slow motion, no knockback or squash. Numbers fade in place without moving or shaking. The low-health pulse glows steadily. FlashSafety , MaxFlashesPerSecond An object flashes at full strength at most 3 times a second (common photosensitivity guidance); extra flashes are dimm"},{"p":"2D games","h":"2D games","u":"2d.html#2d-games","x":"Everything works in 2D the same way. The kit notices a 2D target (a Rigidbody 2D, or sprites and no 3D Rigidbody) and: flashes the sprite's own shape (sorted just above it, following its sprite animation, flip and sorting layer), pushes along the hit in the X and Y plane with an upward part, using the Rigidbody 2D (or the transform), shakes and kicks an orthographic camera (the zoom kick changes its size), shows numbers and bars as in 3D (Screen space keeps them the same size on screen), points the player's damage arrow by screen up and right. Tip: put Hit Feedback on the root and set Visual t"},{"p":"Why No Feedback? and Scene Overview","h":"Why No Feedback? and Scene Overview","u":"why-no-feedback.html#why-no-feedback-and-scene-overview","x":""},{"p":"Why No Feedback? and Scene Overview","h":"Why No Feedback?","u":"why-no-feedback.html#why-no-feedback","x":"Tools > All In One Hit Feedback Kit > Why No Feedback? (also the button in the Hit Feedback inspector). In Play Mode it shows the selected object (or the last one hit) with its eight most recent hits. For each hit: the kind, amount, profile and strength, then every part (number, flash, hitstop, camera shake, knockback, squash, health bar, sound, particles, kill moment) as played or skipped , with the reason in plain words. The latest hit has a one-click fix where there is one: Reason Fix Off on this object Use Profile for that part No camera (tag one MainCamera) Tag the camera as MainCamera No"},{"p":"Why No Feedback? and Scene Overview","h":"Scene Overview","u":"why-no-feedback.html#scene-overview","x":"Tools > All In One Hit Feedback Kit > Scene Overview lists every Hit Feedback in the open scenes with its preset or profile, what it found (renderers, what pushes it, Animator, health) and any problems, plus the player feedback, screen bars and the camera. Click a name to select it."},{"p":"Why No Feedback? and Scene Overview","h":"From code","u":"why-no-feedback.html#from-code","x":"hitFeedback.LastReport , GetReport(i) , HitReport.Reason(effect) , HitReport.Describe(reason) and HitReport.Fix(reason) give you the same information for your own logs or tools."},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Code, events and your own UI","u":"extending.html#code-events-and-your-own-ui","x":"Namespace RomanoAssets.HitFeedbackKit . Everything below is public and documented in the code."},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Hitting","u":"extending.html#hitting","x":"HitFeedback.Hit(target, 25); // GameObject or any Component (a collider you hit) HitFeedback.Hit(target, new HitInfo(25, HitKind.Critical) .At(hitPoint).From(direction).By(attacker).Killing(isKill)); var report = hitFeedback.Play(hitInfo); // what played and why hitFeedback.Freeze(0.1f); // a target-only freeze by itself hitFeedback.StopAll(); // stop everything running on it and put it back"},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Events","u":"extending.html#events","x":"Event When HitFeedback.AnyHit (static) Every hit on any target: your combo counter, achievements, logs. hitFeedback.Played Every hit on this target, with its report. OnHit (UnityEvent with the amount), OnKilled In the inspector, no code. Health.OnDamaged , OnHealed , OnDied , Changed Health changes."},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Your own health","u":"extending.html#your-own-health","x":"public class MyHealth : MonoBehaviour, IHealthSource { public float hp = 100, maxHp = 100; public float CurrentHealth => hp; public float MaxHealth => maxHp; public float CurrentShield => 0; public float MaxShield => 0; }"},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Your own number display (TextMesh Pro, UI Toolkit)","u":"extending.html#your-own-number-display-textmesh-pro-ui-toolkit","x":"public class MyNumbers : MonoBehaviour, IDamageNumberDisplay { void OnEnable() => DamageNumbers.CustomDisplay = this; void OnDisable() { if (DamageNumbers.CustomDisplay == (IDamageNumberDisplay)this) DamageNumbers.CustomDisplay = null; } public int Show(Vector3 worldPosition, string text, HitKind kind, Color color, float sizeInPixels, Sprite icon, int combineWith, HitFeedback owner) { // Spawn your TextMeshPro popup here. The text is ready: \"1.2K\", \"+25\", \"Miss\". return 0; } } Your own display gets a string per number (the kit's own numbers make none)."},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Your scripts in hitstop","u":"extending.html#your-scripts-in-hitstop","x":"public class MyAI : MonoBehaviour, IHitstopReceiver { bool frozen; public void OnHitstop(bool isFrozen) => frozen = isFrozen; void Update() { if (frozen) return; /* ... */ } }"},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Global settings, time and the screen","u":"extending.html#global-settings-time-and-the-screen","x":"HitFeedbackSettings (accessibility, master juice, volume, rumble, camera, Translate for your localisation), TimeControl.Request(scale, seconds) and StopAll() (safe slow motion), ScreenFlash.Play(color, amount) , DamageNumbers.Show / ShowText / Clear / Hidden / Prewarm , HitFeedbackOverlay.Visible (hide the screen layer behind your menus) and HitCameraShake.For(camera).AddTrauma(amount, settings) ."},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Multiplayer","u":"extending.html#multiplayer","x":"Feedback is local to each player's screen, so there is nothing to sync: play the hit on every client from your own networked event (an RPC or a synced health change). With Netcode for GameObjects, call HitFeedback.Hit in the ClientRpc (or OnValueChanged of a health NetworkVariable) that tells clients about the hit."},{"p":"Code, events and your own UI","h":"Performance","u":"extending.html#performance","x":"Numbers, bars and the player's screen effects are drawn in a few batched calls. Hits, numbers and bars make no garbage in steady play (checked by the kit's tests and in built players). Call DamageNumbers.Prewarm() while loading to make the number pool up front."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Upgrading","u":"upgrading.html#upgrading","x":""},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"1.0.0","u":"upgrading.html#100","x":"First release. Nothing to do."},{"p":"Upgrading","h":"Updating the kit later","u":"upgrading.html#updating-the-kit-later","x":"Import the new version over the old one. Your profiles, settings and Setup Wizard variants live in Assets/HitFeedbackKitData , which updates never touch. Class names, serialized fields, menu paths and settings keys don't change between versions, so your scenes, prefabs and code keep working. Anything that needs a step from you is listed here. If you moved or renamed the kit's folder, that's fine: the tools find the kit's assets by name, not by path."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","u":"troubleshooting.html#troubleshooting-and-faq","x":"First, open Why No Feedback? It names the reason for almost everything below, with a fix."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Nothing happens when I hit","u":"troubleshooting.html#nothing-happens-when-i-hit","x":"Is there a Hit Feedback on the object, or a parent of the collider you hit? Why No Feedback? catches hits on objects without one. Using the kit's Health? Damage while invulnerable, or after death, plays nothing. Is HitFeedbackSettings.Enabled on, and the master Juice above 0?"},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"No numbers or no shake","u":"troubleshooting.html#no-numbers-or-no-shake","x":"The kit needs a camera: the one tagged MainCamera , or set HitFeedbackSettings.Camera (or the camera tag in Project Settings). Numbers farther than Max Distance aren't shown."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Numbers show over my pause menu / under my UI","u":"troubleshooting.html#numbers-show-over-my-pause-menu--under-my-ui","x":"The screen layer is drawn after the frame. It hides by itself while the game is paused ( Time.timeScale at 0). For other menus set HitFeedbackOverlay.Visible = false while they are open. Your own OnGUI windows draw on top of it."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"The flash doesn't show","u":"troubleshooting.html#the-flash-doesnt-show","x":"Static batched objects can't flash: untick Batching Static (Why No Feedback? has the button). Particle, trail and line renderers aren't flashed. Added renderers at runtime? Call RebuildFlash() ."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Knockback does nothing","u":"troubleshooting.html#knockback-does-nothing","x":"The object needs something to move: a non-kinematic Rigidbody or Rigidbody 2D, a Character Controller, a NavMesh Agent, or Move Transform turned on. Kinematic bodies are moved like transforms."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"My camera controller and the shake","u":"troubleshooting.html#my-camera-controller-and-the-shake","x":"They don't fight: the shake is added after your controller runs and removed before it runs again. If you shake a camera rig instead, add Hit Camera Shake to the camera and set its Shake Target."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Squash changes my collider","u":"troubleshooting.html#squash-changes-my-collider","x":"Set Hit Feedback > Visual to a child that holds only the model."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Slow motion stayed on","u":"troubleshooting.html#slow-motion-stayed-on","x":"It can't: the game's own time scale always comes back, and if your game changes Time.timeScale (a pause menu) the kit lets go at once. TimeControl.StopAll() ends every stop now."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Is it multiplayer ready?","u":"troubleshooting.html#is-it-multiplayer-ready","x":"Feedback is local; play it on each client from your networked hit event. See Code ."},{"p":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","h":"Known limits","u":"troubleshooting.html#known-limits","x":"Numbers and bars are a screen layer, not 3D objects in the scene; \"World\" numbers are sized by distance and hidden behind walls by a raycast. No UI Toolkit or VR-specific feedback in 1.0 (bring your own display through IDamageNumberDisplay )."},{"p":"Support","h":"Support","u":"support.html#support","x":"Need help? Every message gets an answer. Documentation: https://romanoassets.github.io/hit-feedback-kit/docs/ Discord: https://discord.gg/AmpBDxRAKu (ask in #help) Report a bug: https://github.com/romanoassets/RomanoAssetSupport/issues Website: https://romanoassets.github.io/hit-feedback-kit/ Email: marklromano@outlook.com In Unity: Tools > All In One Hit Feedback Kit > Help and Support , then Copy Support Info , and paste it in your message. It copies the kit version, Unity version, render pipeline, platform and graphics API. Enjoying the kit? A short review on the Asset Store helps a lot."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"Changelog","u":"changelog.html#changelog","x":"All notable changes to the All In One Hit Feedback Kit. The format follows Keep a Changelog; versions follow Semantic Versioning."},{"p":"Changelog","h":"[1.0.0] - 2026-11-08","u":"changelog.html#100---2026-11-08","x":"First release."}];
